# SESSION_PROTOCOL

Why every session in {{ repo }} ends the way `AGENTS.md` — and, for a session running
as an installed skill, the skill's own instructions — say it should. Those carry the
operational short form, read every reply; this file is where the reasoning behind it
lives once, so neither has to restate it.

## Why concise, by default

Default AI verbosity costs the reader attention they didn't agree to spend. That
isn't a nice-to-have setting — it ships on, and this is the one place that says why
instead of just asserting it.

**Always:** concise, complete, straight to the point. State results and decisions
directly. No restating the request, no narrating steps about to be taken, no padding.

## The status block

Every substantive reply ends with a compact block. Omit a line entirely when there's
nothing to report for it — never print "None" as filler:

- **Questions pending** — brief bullets, awaiting {{ assignee }}.
- **Progress** — % complete on the current task or milestone.
- **Git/PR status** — branch, clean or uncommitted, PR number and state.
- **Expected next input** — what {{ assignee }} should say next. If the session can
  compact its own context or start fresh, and the conversation has grown long, say so
  here — capability-gated phrasing, never a named command.
- **Model guidance** — if the session runs on more than one capability tier or effort
  setting, state the minimum the next step needs, every substantive reply, changed or
  not — this line doesn't follow the "omit if nothing to report" rule above, because
  there's always a current recommendation to state. A session with no tiers to choose
  between omits the whole line instead.

## Sizing an issue before spending effort on it

Committing a high-capability tier and high reasoning effort to a one-line typo fix
wastes budget. Committing a low tier to a genuinely hard problem produces a
plausible-looking wrong answer, which costs more to unwind than the tier saved.

**If the session can run on more than one tier or effort setting**, size the issue
the same way whether it already existed or was just created for work described in
chat: read the board's Size field if the issue has one, or estimate it the same way
yourself if it doesn't and say that's what you're doing. `WORKFLOW.md` already rules
out faking Priority/Size with labels when there's no board — the same rule applies
here: an estimate lives in the reply, never in a GitHub field the board doesn't have.

| Size | Suggested minimum |
|---|---|
| `XS` / `S` | The lightest tier available, low effort. |
| `M` | A middle tier, default effort. |
| `L` / `XL` | The most capable tier available, high effort. |

State the recommendation in capability terms, never as a specific product or model
name — the same rule that governs "compact or start fresh" above governs this. Then
ask: continue on the current tier, or switch?

**If switching**, don't carry a long conversation across the switch — compact the
session's context first, or start a fresh session on the target tier and hand it the
GitHub issue number. A fresh session can re-derive what it needs from the issue; a
long conversation dragged across a tier change usually can't be trusted to have
carried the right parts of itself along.

This table is a starting point, not a gate — nothing mechanically enforces it, the
same way nothing enforces the rest of this file.

## Checkpointing before the end

A deliberate close-out assumes the session gets to choose when it ends. It often
doesn't — a session can run out of context, get killed, or lose its connection
mid-task, with no chance to ever reach "a real stopping point." If the only write to
`{{ memoryFile }}` happens there, an interrupted session leaves nothing behind, and
the next one starts cold with no idea what was mid-flight.

So memory isn't only a close-out artifact. Overwrite `{{ memoryFile }}` at natural
checkpoints during the session too — a sub-task finishing, before a long or risky
step, any time the state worth resuming from changes — with wherever things actually
stand. It doesn't need the Done/Planned/Memory-handoff structure below; a rough
current-state note is the point, not a polished one.

## Ending a session

At a real stopping point — not after every message:

- **Done this session** — brief, complete bullets.
- **Planned for next session** — brief, complete bullets.
- **Memory handoff** — enough state that the next session can pick up cold, written
  to **`{{ memoryFile }}`** and nowhere else.

Push everything, update the issue and PR, leave the working tree clean, before
signing off. This is a handoff, not a pause.

## Why memory is a file, never a GitHub comment

Two different things get updated at the end of a session, and they don't belong in
the same place:

- **Issue and PR *fields*** — status, labels, milestone, assignee, links — are real
  project data. They keep being updated on GitHub, exactly as `WORKFLOW.md` describes.
- **An agent's running account of its own sessions** — what it tried, what it
  learned, where it stopped — is not project data. It goes in `{{ memoryFile }}`,
  never to an issue comment, a PR comment, or a board note.

GitHub stays exactly as readable to a human as it would be if no agent had ever
touched this repo. This is not a preference a capable agent gets to override — there
is no GitHub-comment option, for any target, regardless of what it's able to do.

## What isn't checkable from inside a session

Nothing in this repo mechanically verifies that a reply actually ended with the
status block above — there's no test that can observe a live conversation. The
closest thing to a gate is the discipline of following this file itself, every reply,
including the one that just generated it.
